<comment> softmax RD RS1 imm. Hardware format is R-type: opcode[31:26]=111101 | RS1[25:21]=input vector base reg | RS2[20:16]=output vector base reg | RD[15:11]=unused | vector_size[10:0]. Declared as type I here on purpose: the I-type encoding (opcode|RS1|RD|imm16) produces the exact same bits when imm is 2047 or less - the RD operand lands in [20:16] (the R-type RS2 slot) and imm[10:0] is the vector size with imm[15:11]=0. RESA's own R-type cannot encode this format (it would force the fixed function code into bits [5:0], inside vector_size). Keep imm at 2047 max or it corrupts the [15:11] field. Opcode moved from 111111 to 111101 to avoid clashing with halt - dlx_control.v D10_SOFTMAX must be 12'b111101_?????? to match. </comment>
<command>
<type>I</type>
<opcode>111101</opcode>
<name>softmax</name>
<function>000000</function>
<action>nop</action>
<args>RD,RS1,imm</args>
<isbranch>false</isbranch>
</command>
<comment> mult - Q16.16 fixed-point multiply. Hardware computes dest := (src1*src2) &gt;&gt; 16 (bits [47:16] of the 64-bit signed product; multiplier module in multiplayer.v, 3-cycle S_MULT stall in dlx_control.v, decode D14_MULT = 12'b111110_?????? ignores bits [10:0]). Hardware word: opcode[31:26]=111110 | src1[25:21] | src2[20:16] | dest[15:11] | 0. Declared type I like softmax because RESA's R-type encoder hardcodes opcode[31:26]=000000 and ignores the opcode element (verified 2026-07: a type-R mult entry emitted 0x00261000 = a shift). The I-type encoding (opcode | RS1&lt;&lt;21 | RD&lt;&lt;16 | imm) produces the exact hardware word when written as: mult SRC2 SRC1 imm, with imm = DEST register number * 0x800 (= DEST&lt;&lt;11). Example: dest R2 = R1 * R6 is written "mult R6 R1 0x1000" and emits 0xF8261000 (R2*0x800 = 0x1000; R3-&gt;0x1800, R4-&gt;0x2000, ... R31-&gt;0xF800). NOTE the operand order: first operand is the SECOND SOURCE, the destination is the immediate. Writing three registers ("mult R2 R1 R6") now fails loudly instead of miscompiling. action is nop because RESA's simulator cannot express the &gt;&gt;16 - like sigmoid/tanh/gelu/softmax, real results come only from the FPGA. </comment>
<command>
<type>I</type>
<opcode>111110</opcode>
<name>mult</name>
<function>000000</function>
<action>nop</action>
<args>RD,RS1,imm</args>
<isbranch>false</isbranch>
</command>
